1
00:00:01,300 --> 00:00:06,622
[SPEAKER_01]: This is a question on practice for lay people.

2
00:00:06,682 --> 00:00:14,465
[SPEAKER_01]: How should a lay person practice, if their goal is nibbāna?

3
00:00:15,966 --> 00:00:19,428
[SPEAKER_00]: Or at least some stage towards it, I presume.

4
00:00:20,188 --> 00:00:22,949
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can't attain nibbāna as a lay person.

5
00:00:25,350 --> 00:00:26,671
[SPEAKER_01]: The suttas are pretty clear on that.

6
00:00:27,951 --> 00:00:29,932
[SPEAKER_00]: But that doesn't mean you can't practice towards it.

7
00:00:32,388 --> 00:00:42,817
[SPEAKER_00]: So, well, you would practice in the same way the monks do, just on the basis of a greater compromise.

8
00:00:43,597 --> 00:00:44,778
[SPEAKER_00]: Compromise of a household life.

9
00:00:47,540 --> 00:00:57,749
[SPEAKER_00]: But the practice does not somehow miraculously become different, because you can't leave your home, or you can't leave your family, or you have a duty towards it.

10
00:00:58,353 --> 00:01:01,675
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you can do a different thing towards the same goal of nibbāna.

11
00:01:01,735 --> 00:01:01,896
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

12
00:01:02,156 --> 00:01:03,196
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still the same practice.

13
00:01:04,658 --> 00:01:12,163
[SPEAKER_00]: So, often it can feel like a... like a high standard for a lay person to achieve.

14
00:01:13,824 --> 00:01:23,271
[SPEAKER_00]: Like keeping the eight precepts, celibate, practice towards the right view, or maybe even non-return.

15
00:01:23,991 --> 00:01:24,852
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that's possible.

16
00:01:24,892 --> 00:01:26,493
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that was in the suttas recorded.

17
00:01:27,720 --> 00:01:41,610
[SPEAKER_00]: But what also always those... what those suttas would always also say was quite clearly that in order for one to practice successfully, whether a monk or a layperson, he needs to understand the peril of sensuality.

18
00:01:42,170 --> 00:01:49,095
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind needs to be free from hindrances on the basis of thorough virtue and sense restraint.

19
00:01:49,975 --> 00:01:55,359
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, the difference basically was only... it wasn't in the path they were applying themselves to,

20
00:01:56,269 --> 00:02:02,990
[SPEAKER_00]: The difference was simply in the circumstances they were keeping them back, but the practice was the same.

21
00:02:04,151 --> 00:02:19,234
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if it feels like a high, like a tall order, well, it's normal, it's going to feel that way, and it's fine to feel that way, but it's important to not then try to devalue or lower the threshold.

22
00:02:20,154 --> 00:02:23,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, because it just seems so unattainable, so give me something simpler.

23
00:02:24,395 --> 00:02:24,575
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

24
00:02:24,855 --> 00:02:25,475
[SPEAKER_00]: Quite the opposite.

25
00:02:26,423 --> 00:02:43,258
[SPEAKER_00]: If actually the goal and the threshold remains high, even your failure to reach it will constitute more successful practice than successfully practicing towards a lower threshold, towards something that's not the beginning of the dhamma, but you were led to believe it is.

26
00:02:43,298 --> 00:02:47,261
[SPEAKER_00]: The good example of that is this, like, you know, it seems to be quite...

27
00:02:47,281 --> 00:02:53,707
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, I don't know how prevalent, but... but I think there are probably quite a few people that, eh...

28
00:02:54,825 --> 00:03:05,533
[SPEAKER_00]: You kind of delusionally believe, you know, whether through their own doing or because they were led astray, that you can keep the five precepts and practice the dhamma as a layperson.

29
00:03:06,454 --> 00:03:07,094
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, no.

30
00:03:08,195 --> 00:03:24,568
[SPEAKER_00]: You look at any sutta, whenever the Buddha taught the five precepts, it was just to get better rebirths, heavenly rebirths, to enjoy sensuality further, instructing the spouses, you know, how to look after each other, how to be faithful, because they're going to get great sensual rewards.

31
00:03:25,578 --> 00:03:32,903
[SPEAKER_00]: It was quite clearly not presented as the Noble Eightfold Path, you know, the one way only towards purification of being, the Satipaṭṭhāna and Noble Eightfold Path.

32
00:03:33,544 --> 00:03:39,849
[SPEAKER_00]: It was clearly presented to those who don't want to practice, who are not interested in practicing.

33
00:03:40,489 --> 00:03:52,798
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, okay, well, still, if you have a degree of virtue on the level of five precepts, a degree of honesty, a degree of reasoning, looking after your family...

34
00:03:52,818 --> 00:03:55,020
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the Buddha was teaching people how to spend their money.

35
00:03:56,928 --> 00:04:04,273
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's something like it's quite delusional to believe that these things somehow also lead you to nibbāna, when the context in the suttas is quite clear.

36
00:04:05,034 --> 00:04:22,386
[SPEAKER_00]: However, whenever there were lay people, quite, you know, the foremost, nāndamātā, the foremost in jhānas, a lay woman, all the other householders, they had many disciples, they were all fully established in virtue, eight precepts, so the parallels of sensuality,

37
00:04:23,040 --> 00:04:24,821
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensory strength, all of that.

38
00:04:25,382 --> 00:04:26,883
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how they got where they were.

39
00:04:27,983 --> 00:04:29,764
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you also had lay people in the suttas.

40
00:04:30,485 --> 00:04:34,447
[SPEAKER_00]: There were sotāpānas, or sakadāgāmīs.

41
00:04:34,868 --> 00:04:37,369
[SPEAKER_00]: But they were not... they were living by five precepts.

42
00:04:37,529 --> 00:04:38,430
[SPEAKER_00]: But that was after.

43
00:04:40,011 --> 00:04:41,412
[SPEAKER_00]: After they got their right view.

44
00:04:41,833 --> 00:04:53,661
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they were lucky enough to talk to the Buddha and the Buddha to guide them step by step, then they went back home and were content with their attainment, and then kept the five precepts and remained being a good person.

45
00:04:54,502 --> 00:04:57,864
[SPEAKER_00]: But they were clearly not practicing further.

46
00:04:58,124 --> 00:05:05,950
[SPEAKER_00]: And when the Buddha did instruct, for example, Anantapindika, you know, the famous instruction, before he even started talking anything about the dhamma to him,

47
00:05:06,811 --> 00:05:31,403
[SPEAKER_00]: He was... he first, obviously, the generosity, the merit that you make, elevated mind, talking about the heavens, the good rebirths, and then he was talking about the perils of sensuality, the absolute deprivation of sensual desires, the... the... the bait, the trap, the suffering, all of that, and then he said... then he... his mind was ready to hear the dhamma, and then he taught in dhamma, he got the right view.

48
00:05:32,723 --> 00:05:35,705
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if today you were taught the dhamma, you're not getting the right view.

49
00:05:35,805 --> 00:05:36,265
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is that?

50
00:05:37,922 --> 00:05:41,866
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because you're not seeing the depth of the perils of sensuality.

51
00:05:42,986 --> 00:05:49,752
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it wasn't taught, generally, to lay people, the dhamma, for, who knows, half of half of the Buddha's life, probably.

52
00:05:50,473 --> 00:05:51,374
[SPEAKER_00]: Quite a few decades.

53
00:05:51,454 --> 00:05:53,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, because it says in the sutras, we do not teach this.

54
00:05:53,456 --> 00:05:54,857
[SPEAKER_00]: We do not teach this.

55
00:05:54,897 --> 00:05:56,258
[SPEAKER_00]: No, because they would leave.

56
00:05:56,498 --> 00:05:57,760
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, it's incompatible.

57
00:05:58,981 --> 00:05:59,141
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

58
00:05:59,997 --> 00:06:03,480
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, obviously, as I said, the people have all this access to the suttas and so on.

59
00:06:03,941 --> 00:06:15,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Plus, let's not even get into, you know, the whole idea of you can practice meditation and meditation techniques that somehow lead you to the meat of the practice on the basis of your five precepts or something.

60
00:06:15,591 --> 00:06:17,012
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no such sutta at all.

61
00:06:18,145 --> 00:06:33,531
[SPEAKER_00]: And even satipaṭṭhāna, as we said before, or ānāpānasati, was taught to noble disciples, or if they were just short of noble disciples, it was still taught to the monks, at least, who were fully ready for that, through sense restraint and virtue, in terms of the gradual training.

62
00:06:33,731 --> 00:06:39,693
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this whole view that you can kind of be a householder,

63
00:06:41,578 --> 00:06:49,504
[SPEAKER_00]: Keep the five precepts, you know, do your best in that regard, and do your meditation technique, and somehow that constitutes your practice for dhamma.

64
00:06:49,824 --> 00:06:50,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Just think about it.

65
00:06:51,585 --> 00:06:56,289
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if monks who would have given up a lot more,

66
00:06:58,180 --> 00:07:08,688
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to do all these other things in terms of the Buddha's instruction, you know, the detailed instruction, and repetitive, and constantly having to remind them, and a right sense of urgency.

67
00:07:09,028 --> 00:07:12,451
[SPEAKER_00]: They still need to do a lot on the basis of already having done a lot.

68
00:07:12,831 --> 00:07:17,314
[SPEAKER_00]: Giving up the household, the attachments, the bonds, all of that.

69
00:07:18,075 --> 00:07:19,035
[SPEAKER_00]: But they need to do a lot.

70
00:07:19,756 --> 00:07:23,119
[SPEAKER_00]: But then you have somebody who hasn't even given all of that up, and needs to do even less.

71
00:07:24,288 --> 00:07:25,328
[SPEAKER_00]: For the same practice.

72
00:07:26,049 --> 00:07:28,450
[SPEAKER_00]: That's simply irrational, even if you think about it.

73
00:07:28,490 --> 00:07:31,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Put aside that the suttas simply don't support that idea.

74
00:07:31,851 --> 00:07:32,751
[SPEAKER_00]: Just stop and think about it.

75
00:07:32,791 --> 00:07:33,952
[SPEAKER_00]: Surely, it would be other way around.

76
00:07:35,152 --> 00:07:47,337
[SPEAKER_00]: Surely, if somebody would not have given up the big part of obstruction for the goal of the holy life, would need to do then even a lot more, just to compensate for that compromise.

77
00:07:47,937 --> 00:07:52,259
[SPEAKER_00]: And somebody who would have given it already up and lives already in a higher kind of

78
00:07:54,380 --> 00:07:58,443
[SPEAKER_00]: In a higher environment of sensory strain, well, they would need... they would need to do less.

79
00:07:59,884 --> 00:08:04,028
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the point is, if you feel disheartened, oh, but it's just so much, it's fine.

80
00:08:04,908 --> 00:08:07,891
[SPEAKER_00]: But don't obscure the fact that it is... that's the threshold.

81
00:08:08,992 --> 00:08:19,480
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you sort of calm down and come to terms with it, if you still keep trying towards that which feels too much, you are actually still getting closer to it, even through your failures.

82
00:08:20,488 --> 00:08:25,750
[SPEAKER_00]: So, keep the five precepts, and then keep the eight precepts every now and then, but keep them really unconditionally.

83
00:08:27,670 --> 00:08:38,434
[SPEAKER_00]: And don't look for meditation techniques to compensate for your intentionally chosen unsuitable environment, but take responsibility for it.

84
00:08:39,874 --> 00:08:42,475
[SPEAKER_00]: And then feel the weight of that choice, feel the weight of the compromise.

85
00:08:47,217 --> 00:08:48,177
[SPEAKER_00]: And through doing that,

86
00:08:48,923 --> 00:08:50,584
[SPEAKER_00]: You are actually on the right track.

87
00:08:51,765 --> 00:09:03,495
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you never become a monk, you are still practicing more rightly than somebody who would have lowered the threshold just because the threshold they hear upsets them.

88
00:09:03,535 --> 00:09:07,979
[SPEAKER_00]: It makes them feel bad, or it makes them feel disheartened, and they don't want to do anything.

89
00:09:10,281 --> 00:09:11,422
[SPEAKER_00]: No, the threshold remains the same.

90
00:09:12,042 --> 00:09:12,603
[SPEAKER_00]: Think about it.

91
00:09:12,643 --> 00:09:13,404
[SPEAKER_00]: If I basically...

92
00:09:14,845 --> 00:09:17,427
[SPEAKER_00]: If there is a peak of the mountain you have to climb to,

93
00:09:19,865 --> 00:09:20,686
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not negotiable.

94
00:09:20,886 --> 00:09:21,506
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the peak.

95
00:09:24,828 --> 00:09:31,973
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you don't aim in the direction of that peak, because it's too much, and start walking in the opposite direction, you're not getting closer to the peak.

96
00:09:32,714 --> 00:09:36,297
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you climb other mountains, it's not the one you should be climbing.

97
00:09:38,858 --> 00:09:41,980
[SPEAKER_00]: So the point is to recognize that, yeah, it feels too high.

98
00:09:43,021 --> 00:09:44,022
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't even know I can do it.

99
00:09:45,073 --> 00:09:54,603
[SPEAKER_00]: But, at least if I don't lower it down, just to please myself, my ignorant self, my aims will be aiming high where they should.

100
00:09:55,464 --> 00:09:59,888
[SPEAKER_00]: And, you know, when you aim high, even when you fail, you fail relatively higher than you were before.

101
00:10:01,470 --> 00:10:04,193
[SPEAKER_00]: And same, same principle in education or in any other field.

102
00:10:05,334 --> 00:10:07,096
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if you aim for, for like a

103
00:10:08,988 --> 00:10:16,192
[SPEAKER_00]: Higher goal, or a more, kind of, accurate, sort of, higher purpose, even your failure will be a relative success.

104
00:10:17,352 --> 00:10:23,175
[SPEAKER_00]: But if I lower the threshold and tell you, you know, just do this, well, you're going to aim even lower now.

105
00:10:23,996 --> 00:10:25,476
[SPEAKER_00]: You're going to fall even lower than that.

106
00:10:27,617 --> 00:10:29,098
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the whole point.

107
00:10:29,118 --> 00:10:31,600
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not like all this insistence on sensitivity and stuff.

108
00:10:31,920 --> 00:10:34,221
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not just to dishearten and, oh, this is just for the monks.

109
00:10:34,261 --> 00:10:36,562
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's for anybody who wants to practice the dhamma.

110
00:10:36,622 --> 00:10:38,223
[SPEAKER_00]: And this is the beginning of the dhamma.

111
00:10:40,095 --> 00:10:42,897
[SPEAKER_00]: If you read the suttas, you will arrive at the very same conclusion.

112
00:10:43,097 --> 00:10:44,458
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't have to take my word for it.

113
00:10:45,319 --> 00:10:49,502
[SPEAKER_00]: And even like, as I said, Nanda Mata that I mentioned, you know, she was like anāgāmī.

114
00:10:50,223 --> 00:10:55,607
[SPEAKER_00]: And she, when she talked about herself, she said, and she had a husband, and she had a kid, before.

115
00:10:56,968 --> 00:11:08,077
[SPEAKER_00]: But she said, even then, since she was a young girl, when she was given to her husband, you know, in marriage, she was never unfaithful to him, even in her thought.

116
00:11:09,815 --> 00:11:11,116
[SPEAKER_00]: Let alone in the body of speech.

117
00:11:12,497 --> 00:11:17,021
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize that she was already quite a developed human being.

118
00:11:17,041 --> 00:11:22,165
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, how many people today can say that even mentally they don't transgress?

119
00:11:23,086 --> 00:11:24,747
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't have a thought of sensuality?

120
00:11:26,208 --> 00:11:28,169
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize she was dutiful.

121
00:11:28,809 --> 00:11:29,849
[SPEAKER_00]: She bore him a kid.

122
00:11:30,109 --> 00:11:32,690
[SPEAKER_00]: When he died, she immediately became celibate.

123
00:11:33,010 --> 00:11:36,691
[SPEAKER_00]: She was simply not interested in any of that since way before.

124
00:11:37,451 --> 00:11:45,294
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when her own son was imprisoned, falsely imprisoned, falsely accused, killed, she didn't go and fight the king for injustice.

125
00:11:45,334 --> 00:11:49,755
[SPEAKER_00]: She said her mind remained unchanged and then decided to put the praise there.

126
00:11:49,775 --> 00:11:53,256
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, wow, you can purify thoughts in the manifestations.

127
00:11:53,376 --> 00:11:55,637
[SPEAKER_00]: As they manifest, they're already clear.

128
00:11:56,843 --> 00:11:59,485
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, yeah, a lay person can do that.

129
00:12:00,766 --> 00:12:02,327
[SPEAKER_00]: And even back then, that was impressive.

130
00:12:02,347 --> 00:12:05,049
[SPEAKER_00]: There were monks who couldn't do that.

131
00:12:05,069 --> 00:12:08,851
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, it's not about, oh, you must become a monk or a nun.

132
00:12:09,772 --> 00:12:20,059
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about, you mustn't lower the threshold, and then keep trying towards that level, and understand that even if you're failing, you're failing in regard to the right direction.

133
00:12:20,119 --> 00:12:21,820
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you are actually going forward.

134
00:12:22,741 --> 00:12:26,003
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe not as fast as you would like to, or as others would promise you,

135
00:12:27,090 --> 00:12:28,310
[SPEAKER_00]: But you are going forward.

136
00:12:29,891 --> 00:12:38,874
[SPEAKER_00]: Through that repetition of failure of the right aim, you are climbing.

137
00:12:38,954 --> 00:12:43,756
[SPEAKER_01]: So, some people might say that, okay, well, I'm not so good with my virtue.

138
00:12:44,276 --> 00:12:47,217
[SPEAKER_01]: I keep five pieces every now and then, eight pieces every now and then.

139
00:12:48,078 --> 00:12:49,098
[SPEAKER_01]: I fail at it.

140
00:12:51,775 --> 00:12:58,701
[SPEAKER_01]: But, I compensate that with meditation technique, meditation whatever.

141
00:12:59,181 --> 00:13:04,826
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, well, you have basically the whole Visuddhimāga commentaries, the whole Abhidharma to blame for that.

142
00:13:05,566 --> 00:13:11,491
[SPEAKER_00]: That was a complete invention when they tried to popularize Buddhism for masses.

143
00:13:12,518 --> 00:13:26,364
[SPEAKER_00]: I think I told you, I think it was a buddhagosa who said, the the Visuddhimāga compiler, who said he hopes that his work will inspire people, whereby even common householders will speak about meditation on a daily basis, because it's a, you know...

144
00:13:26,884 --> 00:13:32,046
[SPEAKER_00]: It became quite associated with the whole national pride, religion, the whole idea of it.

145
00:13:32,266 --> 00:13:41,850
[SPEAKER_00]: And so it's no wonder that virtue had to suffer on account of it, the threshold of virtue, because that's one thing that that your average householder doesn't want to hear, doesn't want to do.

146
00:13:43,287 --> 00:13:55,414
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you lower that and present some... some more instantaneously gratifying yogic, tantric, hypnotic meditation techniques, then you would get probably quite a few followers in that regard.

147
00:13:56,174 --> 00:14:09,042
[SPEAKER_00]: And ever since, even the whole, like, the... the Thai tradition and Boran Kamathan and the Thai forest meditation, you look at the history of it, it just came exactly from these abidamic views mixed with Tibetan and Chinese tantra,

148
00:14:09,962 --> 00:14:15,027
[SPEAKER_00]: And in sort of visualization practices and stuff, and again...

149
00:14:15,407 --> 00:14:19,331
[SPEAKER_00]: Intention for popularizing... Yeah, exactly.

150
00:14:19,391 --> 00:14:20,712
[SPEAKER_00]: Came from the same source.

151
00:14:20,812 --> 00:14:21,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Came from the same source.

152
00:14:21,753 --> 00:14:24,335
[SPEAKER_00]: And then even people with... And see why that's so dangerous.

153
00:14:24,756 --> 00:14:34,825
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, even people who would have committed for right reasons to the practice, if that's where they arrived at, thinking that's what the Buddha actually taught, they wouldn't look further, and they'll be doing this nonsense.

154
00:14:36,042 --> 00:14:40,224
[SPEAKER_00]: Instead of doing the right thing, they were perfectly capable of doing, if they were told.

155
00:14:42,445 --> 00:14:56,691
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the whole... and same in Burma, the whole vipassana movement was told by that minister to the teacher of Mahāsī, like king's minister or something, because, again, he was like... he was very bound with their... with the Buddhist identity, colonial times, the pressures and stuff.

156
00:14:57,211 --> 00:15:05,055
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he was like, let's find the sutta that can serve as a basis, and then created these technique practices, which, by the way, are quite helpful management.

157
00:15:07,072 --> 00:15:13,175
[SPEAKER_00]: Management techniques, like any other sort of practices, you might do to, like, you know, relieve the stress.

158
00:15:13,915 --> 00:15:21,198
[SPEAKER_00]: But confusing that, for the Buddhist practice, it's pretty much confusing, basically, treating of a random symptom for the illness.

159
00:15:21,919 --> 00:15:26,861
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when the symptom disappears, you think you're cured now, while actually your illness is deteriorating.

160
00:15:26,881 --> 00:15:30,643
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's the most perilous thing to do, in that sense.

161
00:15:32,091 --> 00:15:39,217
[SPEAKER_00]: You read the suttas, the instruction to lay people, five precepts, eight precepts when you can, more often better, great.

162
00:15:40,678 --> 00:15:48,645
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, fulfill your other duties, but then at the same time reflect, reflect on the nature of enlightenment, reflect on the Buddha, the dhamma, the saṅgha.

163
00:15:48,885 --> 00:15:52,609
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, it's always presented in that way, you know, through the oral transmission.

164
00:15:52,669 --> 00:15:55,972
[SPEAKER_00]: Lots of details were lost, but you could extrapolate from that and see.

165
00:15:56,765 --> 00:16:00,988
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, what is... what is... what would be the qualities, the properties of an enlightened mind?

166
00:16:01,648 --> 00:16:09,553
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it would be, obviously, disgust with sensuality, freedom from any... any physical cravings, freedom from conceit.

167
00:16:09,593 --> 00:16:14,456
[SPEAKER_00]: So, okay, so all of these contexts, all of these ideas, think about.

168
00:16:14,496 --> 00:16:24,663
[SPEAKER_00]: Sit down for an hour, and instead of watching your belly rising up and down, think about the property of being free from conceit, and try and apply it.

169
00:16:25,749 --> 00:16:31,212
[SPEAKER_00]: On the basis of your whatever limited virtue, limited sense restraint, still work in the same direction.

170
00:16:32,913 --> 00:16:36,635
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, the path does not magically become different just because you can't do this.

171
00:16:38,236 --> 00:16:39,317
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still the same practice.

172
00:16:42,298 --> 00:16:44,439
[SPEAKER_00]: Pick up any other quality, same.

173
00:16:45,120 --> 00:16:48,642
[SPEAKER_00]: Different times, different reflections, but invest time in it.

174
00:16:49,542 --> 00:16:50,703
[SPEAKER_00]: And then try to apply it.

175
00:16:52,642 --> 00:17:00,287
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you end up in situations that provoke your conceit, provoke your lust, provoke your anger, ah, that's the training now.

176
00:17:00,307 --> 00:17:10,433
[SPEAKER_00]: Tame the animal in regard to this, within my compromised environment, for which I have taken up responsibility.

177
00:17:10,453 --> 00:17:20,600
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even... that's something like... and that's why it's... you realize, like, all you can do is say these things, basically, to people, because

178
00:17:21,501 --> 00:17:27,124
[SPEAKER_00]: At least one in hundred, when they hear it, they will be able to do it.

179
00:17:27,384 --> 00:17:30,445
[SPEAKER_00]: And the reason they were able to do it is because they actually heard it.

180
00:17:30,565 --> 00:17:32,266
[SPEAKER_00]: They were told.

181
00:17:32,286 --> 00:17:34,767
[SPEAKER_00]: They can't have an excuse to say, well, I was never told.

182
00:17:36,208 --> 00:17:41,050
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's kind of like the whole direction of what people like to think the practice is.

183
00:17:42,030 --> 00:17:42,931
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not incidental.

184
00:17:42,951 --> 00:17:48,793
[SPEAKER_00]: That kind of... it does go with the general flow of nature, kind of preferences and so on.

185
00:17:49,053 --> 00:17:50,554
[SPEAKER_00]: It kind of just tailors to that.

186
00:17:51,752 --> 00:18:04,719
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if, as I said, even if people were maybe misled, somebody told you this is the practice, unenlightened mind will quite happily leap onto that and proliferate further, and defend it, and so on, and not question it.

187
00:18:05,579 --> 00:18:16,785
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, you might have been misled, but if you would have heard the other alternative, like this, and still irrationally chose this without any questioning, well, now that's your own doing.

188
00:18:17,846 --> 00:18:18,366
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

189
00:18:18,386 --> 00:18:19,727
[SPEAKER_00]: All you can do is keep saying it,

190
00:18:20,678 --> 00:18:27,282
[SPEAKER_00]: Because some people, at least when they hear it, well, they can't just go back to what they were doing before without asking certain questions.

191
00:18:28,303 --> 00:18:33,306
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you start asking them, you'll see how quickly the whole view will dissolve and how much it doesn't add up.

192
00:18:33,966 --> 00:18:37,088
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the suttas are completely unambiguous about it.

193
00:18:37,667 --> 00:18:47,937
[SPEAKER_00]: When the Buddha talked to lay people, when he was telling how to look after the wives, or kings how to rule, it was nothing to do with freedom from nibbāna, noble little path, and the dhamma.

194
00:18:48,197 --> 00:18:51,841
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry, freedom from from saṃsāra, nibbāna, and the dhamma.

195
00:18:52,542 --> 00:18:56,465
[SPEAKER_00]: It was for the heavenly rebirths, for the better life here and now.

196
00:18:56,906 --> 00:18:59,649
[SPEAKER_00]: It was because they clearly didn't want to practice.

197
00:19:00,588 --> 00:19:07,838
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he would not have wasted the practice instruction that would have been misunderstood and misconstrued to somebody not even willing to hear it.

198
00:19:08,379 --> 00:19:15,268
[SPEAKER_00]: And when those lay people did, were instructed, it was exactly on the same basis as the monks were.

199
00:19:18,897 --> 00:19:24,140
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, there's the... keep the five precepts, you'll go to heaven.

200
00:19:24,700 --> 00:19:24,861
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

201
00:19:24,941 --> 00:19:26,261
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll enjoy sensual pleasures there.

202
00:19:26,321 --> 00:19:26,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

203
00:19:27,622 --> 00:19:29,483
[SPEAKER_01]: But then there's the danger of those sensual pleasures.

204
00:19:29,723 --> 00:19:30,024
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

205
00:19:30,044 --> 00:19:31,745
[SPEAKER_00]: And see, there's absolutely nothing wrong with it.

206
00:19:31,785 --> 00:19:32,365
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the point.

207
00:19:33,786 --> 00:19:44,332
[SPEAKER_00]: That becomes a seriously mistaken and perilous position when, for whatever reason, that became understood as the practice of the dhamma as well.

208
00:19:45,073 --> 00:19:45,193
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

209
00:19:45,413 --> 00:19:47,294
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's just delusional, as I said.

210
00:19:52,776 --> 00:20:01,418
[SPEAKER_01]: But there's the idea that the virtue is not... even the five precepts is not important, but the meditation is the core.

211
00:20:01,618 --> 00:20:05,099
[SPEAKER_00]: That is even worse, because... Well, yeah, it's completely upside down.

212
00:20:05,179 --> 00:20:14,141
[SPEAKER_01]: Because even if you keep the five precepts and you're aiming for the heavens, still you are in a way practicing the good path.

213
00:20:14,881 --> 00:20:18,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you're not drifting away, too far away from the starting point.

214
00:20:18,842 --> 00:20:20,122
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not practicing the path.

215
00:20:21,019 --> 00:20:28,682
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as I said before, by keeping the five precepts, which is, what I said, the bare minimum, you're at least, as I said, not too far off.

216
00:20:29,042 --> 00:20:39,987
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you do take the eight precepts, or start to practice sense restraint, or reflect on these right, correct contexts, and so on, you are actually close to where things would be starting.

217
00:20:40,967 --> 00:20:46,830
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're not even keeping the five precepts, and just doing some meditation, or occasionally keeping the precepts when you're on the retreat,

218
00:20:48,185 --> 00:20:53,809
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, you can do whatever you want to manage your suffering and it's absolutely fine.

219
00:20:53,829 --> 00:20:54,689
[SPEAKER_00]: There's really no problem.

220
00:20:54,769 --> 00:21:05,116
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is when all those absolutely worldly misguided, in terms of the dhamma, managements are seen as the practice of the dhamma.

221
00:21:05,256 --> 00:21:06,977
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the worst thing you can do, really.

222
00:21:08,638 --> 00:21:15,122
[SPEAKER_00]: That's even worse than somebody who is making no effort to practice, but at least is not obscuring what the practice actually is.

223
00:21:21,763 --> 00:21:38,555
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you relegate virtue as being unimportant, that meditation insight, meditation experiences, as being the core, then, if you're a monk, or a layperson, it means, you know, what's the point of being monastic?

224
00:21:39,628 --> 00:21:41,009
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, in a way, yeah, exactly.

225
00:21:41,149 --> 00:21:41,529
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

226
00:21:41,549 --> 00:21:47,933
[SPEAKER_00]: Then anybody, if the practice, everybody has access to the practice through meditation technique, everything else is secondary to it.

227
00:21:48,093 --> 00:21:48,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

228
00:21:48,693 --> 00:21:51,795
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you know, it's obviously, it's like the mainstream view.

229
00:21:52,335 --> 00:21:54,837
[SPEAKER_00]: Most people do it and most people say, well, but I find it helpful.

230
00:21:55,757 --> 00:21:56,538
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody doubts that.

231
00:21:56,598 --> 00:21:57,498
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody denies that.

232
00:21:58,699 --> 00:22:05,543
[SPEAKER_00]: But you need to really start looking into where the whole thing came from, because you're not going to find it in the suttas.

233
00:22:06,240 --> 00:22:13,804
[SPEAKER_00]: And whenever people don't say, oh, it comes from this sutta, it's always quite a... quite a leap to make those connections.

234
00:22:14,664 --> 00:22:26,131
[SPEAKER_00]: And then people would sometimes just... just accept it, that it came from the suttas, because it was presented... the suttas were presented to them through these meditation techniques, and they haven't really looked into them and studied them for themselves.

235
00:22:27,151 --> 00:22:32,514
[SPEAKER_00]: But there were... there were books written on the whole origins of the, like, modern jhāna views and stuff.

236
00:22:34,229 --> 00:22:47,564
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe we can even link them, like a couple of... like full-on scholastic books, not just somebody expressing their view, like I'm doing now, and then quote the suttas, which, as I said, are fairly generic, people can interpret them whichever way they want.

237
00:22:47,644 --> 00:22:54,852
[SPEAKER_00]: No, this was like a like a scholarly research on the basis of other research, of other research, full-on, like investigative work,

238
00:22:55,785 --> 00:22:56,985
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's just... there's no doubt.

239
00:22:57,265 --> 00:23:08,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, even people that practice the Buddhaghosa jhāna method and whatnot, that had nothing to do with the Buddhist jhānas, actually, even those scholars know that that didn't come from the suttas.

240
00:23:08,529 --> 00:23:12,890
[SPEAKER_00]: They just accept and like and think Buddhaghosa knew what he was talking about.

241
00:23:13,250 --> 00:23:20,192
[SPEAKER_00]: But there is absolutely no disagreement that that has come, like, 10 centuries later, 6, 11.

242
00:23:21,563 --> 00:23:33,975
[SPEAKER_00]: And by that time, there was a lot, already like three centuries after the Buddha passed away, it was already, you know, by the time those saṅgha councils were coming, it was already different, different takes, yogic influences and whatnot.

243
00:23:34,816 --> 00:23:45,787
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's just to show you that, okay, you can do whatever you want, but if you want to claim it to be the dhamma and the practice for recruiting of the suffering and so on,

244
00:23:46,946 --> 00:24:03,095
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to go back to the suttas, that, as I said, there is quite a wide consensus, the early texts, what they were, and really study them on their own merit, not like interpret them through your own, as I said, you know, breath, massage, tantric technique, and try to read into it.

245
00:24:03,435 --> 00:24:12,200
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, just look what it says, forget what you know, and what's the first thing that comes to your mind when you read an ānāpānasati sutta?

246
00:24:12,640 --> 00:24:15,702
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the first thing that comes to my mind is it wasn't taught to householders.

247
00:24:16,985 --> 00:24:24,568
[SPEAKER_00]: The second thing that comes to my mind, it was taught to people with the right view, or with at least fully established virtue, sense restraint, like beyond any doubt.

248
00:24:25,468 --> 00:24:31,390
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, you want to get in these other subsequent technicalities of jhāna, of ānāpānasati steps?

249
00:24:31,430 --> 00:24:33,851
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you gotta meet these technicalities first.

250
00:24:34,572 --> 00:24:39,193
[SPEAKER_00]: Is my life approximating that of a fully established in virtue, celibacy, sense restraint?

251
00:24:39,934 --> 00:24:43,275
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereby, I could say, well, it does apply to me, or no.

252
00:24:44,450 --> 00:24:47,112
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, you could say, oh, well, my teacher told me it doesn't have to.

253
00:24:47,192 --> 00:24:48,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, we're not talking about your teacher.

254
00:24:48,593 --> 00:24:54,276
[SPEAKER_00]: We're talking about the early Buddhist texts, the suttas, that are, as I said, widely agreed.

255
00:24:54,376 --> 00:24:56,637
[SPEAKER_00]: OK, this is the closest to what the Buddha actually taught.

256
00:24:57,058 --> 00:25:00,560
[SPEAKER_00]: And most likely, it was from the historical figure that did teach these things.

257
00:25:01,961 --> 00:25:10,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Do those texts allow you to be a householder with the occasional five precepts, doing higher satipaṭṭhānas and ānāpānasati and four jhāna?

258
00:25:12,167 --> 00:25:13,788
[SPEAKER_00]: Not a chance, by any stretch.

259
00:25:15,469 --> 00:25:16,330
[SPEAKER_00]: And now you have a choice.

260
00:25:16,590 --> 00:25:19,212
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, you could say, okay, I don't like this.

261
00:25:19,473 --> 00:25:22,395
[SPEAKER_00]: I do prefer my my lay jhāna teacher and I'll follow him.

262
00:25:22,716 --> 00:25:23,136
[SPEAKER_00]: That's fine.

263
00:25:23,636 --> 00:25:32,004
[SPEAKER_00]: But you were told, and there is plenty of evidence to even prove that point, scholastic investigative works and stuff, that those are two different things.

264
00:25:33,904 --> 00:25:39,127
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I would see sometimes people go, oh no, there's the Buddha jhāna, and then there's this jhāna, this Ajahn's jhāna.

265
00:25:39,147 --> 00:25:41,788
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, well, no, there is jhāna, full stop.

266
00:25:42,449 --> 00:25:44,170
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no 16 types of jhāna.

267
00:25:44,910 --> 00:25:50,713
[SPEAKER_00]: All the subsequent jhānas are the ones that fail to understand or attain the Buddha's jhāna, the jhāna from the early texts.

268
00:25:51,333 --> 00:25:53,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, you wouldn't come up with your own practice of jhāna.

269
00:25:55,055 --> 00:25:56,416
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's simply not optional.

270
00:25:57,237 --> 00:26:02,001
[SPEAKER_00]: And if the choice is clear, as I said before in other talks, you might even surprise yourself.

271
00:26:02,281 --> 00:26:04,804
[SPEAKER_00]: You think now, maybe you think, oh, it's just too much to do all that.

272
00:26:04,884 --> 00:26:17,334
[SPEAKER_00]: And once you're clear, once you calm down in your initial shock, because, you know, for 20 years you've been doing it this way, you might surprise yourself and realize, actually, I am kind of doing it like this, rightly now.

273
00:26:17,855 --> 00:26:18,175
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't...

274
00:26:18,495 --> 00:26:21,298
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have, actually, a problem with the sense of strain as much as I thought I did.

275
00:26:22,303 --> 00:26:23,283
[SPEAKER_00]: I could even do it more.

276
00:26:23,403 --> 00:26:24,344
[SPEAKER_00]: I could even do it more.

277
00:26:24,764 --> 00:26:25,984
[SPEAKER_00]: Occasionally, I take on the eight precepts.

278
00:26:26,024 --> 00:26:27,165
[SPEAKER_00]: Let me take it on for longer.

279
00:26:27,705 --> 00:26:34,687
[SPEAKER_00]: Let me now improve, like, the the restraint and the practice in regard to the the speech, the action revolving on speech.

280
00:26:35,147 --> 00:26:42,330
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, let me now, like Nanda Mata, purify thoughts in their manifestation, without relying on any management techniques.

281
00:26:42,630 --> 00:26:44,331
[SPEAKER_00]: As they arise, I see through them.

282
00:26:45,471 --> 00:26:51,453
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, she was a laywoman, with probably lots of duties and stuff and pressures, and she did all of that.

283
00:26:52,595 --> 00:26:57,796
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, if, as a householder, you can become an anāgāmī, that's a pretty good deal.

284
00:26:58,116 --> 00:26:59,617
[SPEAKER_00]: But, again, the practice is the same.

285
00:27:00,557 --> 00:27:11,260
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's another thing, because, like, okay, so, could you become an anāgāmī, somebody who is fully, utterly, completely destroyed, the five lower fetters, it's above sensuality or real will, while practicing sexual activity?

286
00:27:12,885 --> 00:27:23,874
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, put everything, put all the text aside, all the teachings, just use clear reason, just pure logic, straightforward reflection, and see, can you do this and get this as a result of that?

287
00:27:25,075 --> 00:27:31,560
[SPEAKER_00]: And see if that's just wishful thinking and a complete kind of, as I said, misled belief.

288
00:27:32,280 --> 00:27:33,321
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's impossible.

289
00:27:33,841 --> 00:27:40,447
[SPEAKER_00]: By any stretch, if you just stop and think about it, you realize, no, of course, if I want to be beyond sensuality, I have to start emulating that.

290
00:27:41,212 --> 00:27:42,633
[SPEAKER_00]: I have to start not giving in to it.

291
00:27:42,934 --> 00:27:53,984
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like a heroin addict, through shooting up drugs, genuinely believes, if he were to genuinely believe, that he will go beyond his addiction.

292
00:27:54,304 --> 00:27:57,667
[SPEAKER_00]: Through feeding it every single day, he will go beyond it.

293
00:27:59,149 --> 00:28:03,313
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't even need to become a heroin addict to realize that that's not how sensuality works.

294
00:28:05,367 --> 00:28:11,592
[SPEAKER_00]: And somehow, you do have views of, I can just keep the five precepts the best I can, and practice for anāgāmī.

295
00:28:11,752 --> 00:28:12,512
[SPEAKER_01]: The destruction.

296
00:28:12,572 --> 00:28:18,597
[SPEAKER_00]: And I find this secret pleasure hidden in the jhāna, that will then make me abandon the whole sensuality, never look back again.

297
00:28:20,098 --> 00:28:26,043
[SPEAKER_00]: And, again, you look at the actual suttas, and it's a very different picture painted there.

298
00:28:27,664 --> 00:28:32,087
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't get the pleasure of renunciation by not... By not renouncing it.

299
00:28:32,107 --> 00:28:32,307
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

300
00:28:35,142 --> 00:28:35,322
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

301
00:28:38,425 --> 00:28:40,767
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in a way, it's actually like...

302
00:28:40,807 --> 00:28:49,133
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm just imagining, like, if I were... if I were a householder and somebody tells me this, it's like, well, actually, that's kind of better.

303
00:28:51,195 --> 00:28:55,178
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't have to be relying on the magic of techniques and attainments.

304
00:28:55,558 --> 00:28:56,679
[SPEAKER_00]: I had a special experience.

305
00:28:56,739 --> 00:28:57,720
[SPEAKER_00]: I'd say, what does it mean?

306
00:28:57,740 --> 00:28:58,461
[SPEAKER_00]: And stuff like that.

307
00:28:58,541 --> 00:29:00,703
[SPEAKER_00]: And then depend on someone else's word.

308
00:29:02,044 --> 00:29:03,205
[SPEAKER_00]: Keep the five precepts.

309
00:29:03,981 --> 00:29:14,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Keep the eight when you can, do it as often as you can, moderate your engagement and compromise of sensuality and other things, and then you have plenty of opportunities for practice.

310
00:29:16,052 --> 00:29:28,382
[SPEAKER_00]: People will be provoking you to say stuff, to think stuff, and you realize every single day you're served on a plate things that can directly lead to improvement of your citta or defilement of it.

311
00:29:30,284 --> 00:29:31,245
[SPEAKER_00]: But you gotta do the work.

312
00:29:32,212 --> 00:29:35,116
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, yeah, actually, it's quite reasonable.

313
00:29:35,456 --> 00:29:50,776
[SPEAKER_00]: If somebody takes on that kind of work, even if it's five, occasional eight, five, occasional eight, but does all of this, does not mask or manage through techniques and mystical experiences and hypnotic trances, there's a good chance you could get the right view.

314
00:29:51,780 --> 00:29:57,064
[SPEAKER_00]: Or at least, be like on the level of a dhamma nusāri and then get the right view at the moment of death or something.

315
00:29:57,604 --> 00:29:59,025
[SPEAKER_00]: Your mind will be very clean.

316
00:29:59,085 --> 00:30:02,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the mind... the mind is actually being worked properly.

317
00:30:03,308 --> 00:30:09,733
[SPEAKER_00]: As opposed to just hypnotized, distracted, enticed with more sensuality to look away from this sensuality.

318
00:30:09,753 --> 00:30:10,113
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

319
00:30:10,233 --> 00:30:11,574
[SPEAKER_00]: You're taking it on rightly.

320
00:30:12,655 --> 00:30:18,019
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, you're not going to be an arahant, but being a sotāpanna, it's, you know, as the Buddha said himself,

321
00:30:19,017 --> 00:30:25,058
[SPEAKER_00]: In those suttas, it's worth being, what is it, like, stuck by a hundred spears every day for a hundred years.

322
00:30:26,619 --> 00:30:31,320
[SPEAKER_00]: If at the end of that you were to get the sotāpārthi, it would be worth going through that pain.

323
00:30:34,020 --> 00:30:36,361
[SPEAKER_00]: So, nothing's stopping you, really.

324
00:30:37,041 --> 00:30:44,583
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing's stopping anyone, except their wrong views and mistaken, misplaced emphasis on what really the practice is and what's important.

325
00:30:52,072 --> 00:31:00,317
[SPEAKER_01]: Because although that's difficult and it's painful, a wise person would see that this pain is worth...

326
00:31:01,198 --> 00:31:02,358
[SPEAKER_01]: This pain is the practice.

327
00:31:02,398 --> 00:31:03,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, is the practice.

328
00:31:03,259 --> 00:31:09,923
[SPEAKER_01]: Because look at the results would be clarity of mind, steadiness of heart, so to speak.

329
00:31:09,943 --> 00:31:10,283
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

330
00:31:11,305 --> 00:31:24,136
[SPEAKER_01]: And as a lay person, if you are still attached to your family and concerned with your children, your wife, wouldn't it be best if you had a clean mind, clean behavior?

331
00:31:24,337 --> 00:31:28,440
[SPEAKER_00]: A non-clouded perspective in regard to what's best for them?

332
00:31:28,460 --> 00:31:30,122
[SPEAKER_00]: Would that not inspire your own family?

333
00:31:30,762 --> 00:31:34,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Wouldn't you be teaching your children the right way to live?

334
00:31:34,244 --> 00:31:36,165
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're beyond their will, beyond sensual...

335
00:31:36,185 --> 00:31:39,507
[SPEAKER_01]: Isn't that the best thing that you should be doing for your family?

336
00:31:39,907 --> 00:31:41,648
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's also good in other ways, obviously.

337
00:31:41,828 --> 00:31:54,275
[SPEAKER_00]: I usually don't talk about that, because generally it's for like a wide audience, but the Buddha said himself, offering, feeding, helping a virtuous person will be like immeasurable merit.

338
00:31:55,028 --> 00:32:00,253
[SPEAKER_00]: Offering, feeding, helping, sotāpāna, even more, sakadāgāmī, even more, anāgāmī, even more, arahant...

339
00:32:01,994 --> 00:32:19,250
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you yourself are actually the one with developed citta, as a householder, developed citta, developed virtue, on the way for the right view, dhammanusāri, sadhanusāri, committing to it rightly, anything good your wife, kids, friends do to you,

340
00:32:20,052 --> 00:32:22,193
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like you are practicing for the benefit.

341
00:32:22,213 --> 00:32:23,753
[SPEAKER_00]: Animals, they do something good for you.

342
00:32:23,773 --> 00:32:26,914
[SPEAKER_00]: If you are one with a purified citta, they're gonna reap manifold benefit from that.

343
00:32:26,934 --> 00:32:27,575
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were not,

344
00:32:49,225 --> 00:32:50,346
[SPEAKER_00]: It's probably not gonna last for long.

345
00:32:51,586 --> 00:32:53,507
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's beneficial one way or the other.

346
00:32:58,069 --> 00:33:01,030
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you're becoming... And that's what I mean, like, and all this is in the suttas.

347
00:33:02,431 --> 00:33:05,172
[SPEAKER_00]: You really see, like, actually, that is perfectly doable.

348
00:33:07,373 --> 00:33:16,978
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, yes, many people would have valid reasons, you know, already have, you know, a growing family or people that depend on them, you know, maybe not as much as back in the day, in India,

349
00:33:17,345 --> 00:33:19,485
[SPEAKER_00]: When you leave your family means, okay, they're done.

350
00:33:20,206 --> 00:33:24,446
[SPEAKER_00]: But still, there is degree of responsibility and involvement and they can't just now...

351
00:33:25,266 --> 00:33:26,327
[SPEAKER_00]: It's too late, so to speak.

352
00:33:26,367 --> 00:33:29,427
[SPEAKER_00]: It's too late to just give this up and become a monk because all of this...

353
00:33:30,227 --> 00:33:44,630
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's like, okay, but it's not too late to internally start walking in the right direction on the basis of their compromised environment, take on that pain of untamed citta being faced correctly, tame it through virtue, five precepts,

354
00:33:45,574 --> 00:33:47,735
[SPEAKER_00]: They keep you not drifting away from the practice.

355
00:33:48,195 --> 00:33:50,396
[SPEAKER_00]: Eight precepts that align you more with the practice.

356
00:33:51,016 --> 00:33:51,896
[SPEAKER_00]: The right reflections.

357
00:33:52,336 --> 00:33:54,017
[SPEAKER_00]: Even the reflections on your own family.

358
00:33:54,737 --> 00:33:54,937
[SPEAKER_00]: See?

359
00:33:55,237 --> 00:33:57,418
[SPEAKER_00]: You are... your children are your bond.

360
00:33:59,138 --> 00:34:00,139
[SPEAKER_00]: What happens to bonds?

361
00:34:00,339 --> 00:34:01,699
[SPEAKER_00]: What happens to those who are bonded?

362
00:34:01,839 --> 00:34:04,180
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, make your meditation around that every other day.

363
00:34:05,320 --> 00:34:09,882
[SPEAKER_00]: One day, think about the virtue, the value of enlightenment, the value of immovable citta.

364
00:34:09,922 --> 00:34:12,083
[SPEAKER_00]: Another day, think about the petals of sensuality.

365
00:34:12,823 --> 00:34:13,083
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

366
00:34:14,048 --> 00:34:19,630
[SPEAKER_00]: Engage with sensuality when you have to, as I said, in a moderate way, but that still doesn't exempt you from thinking how bad it is.

367
00:34:20,791 --> 00:34:22,311
[SPEAKER_00]: Think about the danger of the bond.

368
00:34:22,831 --> 00:34:24,472
[SPEAKER_00]: Think about your children dying before you.

369
00:34:27,253 --> 00:34:31,234
[SPEAKER_00]: Would your mind remain immoved like Nanda Mata's did?

370
00:34:31,895 --> 00:34:33,175
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, oh, it wouldn't.

371
00:34:33,615 --> 00:34:35,276
[SPEAKER_00]: So I gotta work towards that.

372
00:34:35,496 --> 00:34:37,717
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't need to abandon children in order to do their work.

373
00:34:38,852 --> 00:34:40,572
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's no excuse to not do the work.

374
00:34:40,592 --> 00:34:41,152
[SPEAKER_00]: That's my point.

375
00:34:41,252 --> 00:34:42,473
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you say, I can't become a monk.

376
00:34:42,553 --> 00:34:43,893
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, still have to do the same work.

377
00:34:45,853 --> 00:34:47,794
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I can't become celibate because I have duty towards wife.

378
00:34:47,874 --> 00:34:48,354
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, fine.

379
00:34:48,894 --> 00:34:53,675
[SPEAKER_00]: Still think those same themes, contexts, and contemplations.

380
00:34:55,195 --> 00:34:59,496
[SPEAKER_00]: They will keep you in check, but it's going to be unpleasant, because obviously you're in them and thinking them.

381
00:34:59,556 --> 00:35:01,056
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, but that is your training there.

382
00:35:01,856 --> 00:35:06,037
[SPEAKER_00]: If you abandon that and become a monk, as in that environment and become a monk, you're going to have different types of

383
00:35:07,197 --> 00:35:08,558
[SPEAKER_00]: Unpleasantness to think about.

384
00:35:09,759 --> 00:35:14,183
[SPEAKER_00]: But one way or the other, it has to come, because we all start with an untamed citta.

385
00:35:15,084 --> 00:35:21,369
[SPEAKER_00]: We all start with a wild animal that does need to be reined in, and then fed these perceptions, as the Sutta says.

386
00:35:22,250 --> 00:35:24,892
[SPEAKER_00]: Kept in front of it, until it sees for itself.

